<?php declare(strict_types=1); $caseId='TW-CLAIM-000002'; ?><!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1.0"><meta name="description" content="Trust-Worthy Truth Trial 000002 investigates whether Jesus taught the modern Romans Road or sinner's-prayer formula of salvation."><title>Trust-Worthy Case 000002 | What did Jesus teach about salvation?</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="/truth/truth-worthy.css"></head><body><header class="topbar"><div class="wrap nav"><a class="brand" href="/">PROJECT <span>UNVEILED</span></a><nav class="navlinks"><a href="/truth/">Truth Trials</a><a href="/book/">Read</a><a href="#challenge">Challenge</a></nav></div></header><main><section class="hero"><div class="wrap"><div class="eyebrow">Trust-Worthy AI · Truth Trial 000002</div><h1>Did Jesus teach the modern “Romans Road / sinner's prayer” concept of salvation?</h1><p>We are putting a familiar evangelical formula on trial without assuming either that tradition is wrong or that familiarity proves it biblical.</p><div class="manifesto"><strong>Question under test:</strong> Is the modern formula itself Jesus' stated method of salvation, or is it a later evangelistic framework built from genuine New Testament themes?</div></div></section><section class="section"><div class="wrap grid"><article class="card"><div class="case-head"><div><div class="case-id"><?php echo $caseId; ?></div><h2>Provisional finding</h2></div><div class="status">OPEN TO CHALLENGE</div></div><div class="finding"><strong>Evidence presently favors:</strong> The modern standardized sinner's-prayer / Romans-Road presentation is a later evangelistic method, not a formula recorded on Jesus' lips. That does <em>not</em> establish that repentance, faith, confession, grace, or calling on the Lord are unbiblical. It means the modern package must not be confused with a verbatim salvation formula taught by Jesus.</div><h3>What the earliest layer actually shows</h3><p>In the Synoptic traditions Jesus announces the Kingdom, calls people to repent, believe the good news, follow him, love God and neighbor, forgive, show mercy, and live as disciples. When directly asked about eternal life, the surviving accounts do not record Jesus leading the questioner through a standardized conversion prayer. The rich ruler tradition instead moves through commandments, attachment to wealth, following Jesus, the Kingdom, and God's ability to save.</p><p>That makes a narrow historical claim defensible: <strong>we have not found the modern sinner's-prayer script in the recorded teaching of Jesus.</strong> A broader theological claim—what precisely constitutes salvation—requires the whole New Testament and competing interpretations, so Trust-Worthy will not collapse that larger question into one proof text.</p><h3>Romans is evidence, but “Romans Road” is an arrangement</h3><p>Romans contains major Christian claims about sin, grace, faith, confession, justification, Christ, and salvation. The question is not whether Romans matters. The question is whether selecting several verses and arranging them as a step-by-step conversion method is identical to Paul's own argument or to Jesus' teaching. Those are different propositions.</p><h3>Historical test</h3><p>A 2001 Southern Baptist Theological Seminary dissertation by Paul H. Chitwood traces the standardized Sinner's Prayer to modern evangelistic development, arguing that it arose from nineteenth- and early-twentieth-century efforts to simplify reproducible evangelism and was popularized especially through Billy Graham. Other historians identify older “receive Christ” language, so the history is not as simple as saying nobody before the twentieth century ever prayed for conversion. The important distinction is between ancient repentance/faith/prayer and the later standardized conversion technique.</p><h3>Strongest counterargument</h3><p>Defenders can reasonably argue that no one claims Jesus had to recite the exact modern wording: the prayer is intended as a practical expression of New Testament repentance, faith, confession, and calling on the Lord. Romans 10 therefore matters even if the modern presentation is later. This objection substantially weakens any claim that the prayer is inherently false merely because its standardized form is recent.</p><h3>Christ-consistency analysis</h3><p>The recorded Jesus repeatedly moves beyond verbal profession toward allegiance, discipleship, mercy, love, obedience, changed priorities, and fruit. That weighs against treating a spoken formula as a guaranteed transaction detached from the life that follows. It does not by itself prove that praying at conversion is wrong.</p><h3>What survives the first pass</h3><div class="labels"><span class="label">Strong: modern formula is later</span><span class="label">Strong: Jesus did not record a canned prayer</span><span class="label">Strong: repentance/faith are NT themes</span><span class="label">Open: exact soteriological synthesis</span></div><p class="muted">This finding is provisional. A successful challenge can revise it. Bring early evidence for a standardized salvation prayer, a better reading of the Gospel material, evidence that the historical lineage is wrong, or a logical flaw in the distinction between biblical concepts and later method.</p></article><aside class="card"><h3>Evidence map</h3><ul class="rules"><li>Mark 1:14–15 — Kingdom, repentance, belief.</li><li>Matthew 19 / Mark 10 / Luke 18 — eternal life, discipleship, wealth, God's saving action.</li><li>Luke 10:25–28 — eternal-life question in a Torah/love-of-neighbor setting.</li><li>Romans 3, 6, 10 — major Pauline material used by modern Romans-Road presentations.</li><li>Historical evangelism — distinguish ancient prayer/repentance from a standardized conversion script.</li><li>Chitwood (2001) — dedicated historical/theological study arguing recent standardized origin.</li></ul><h3 style="margin-top:28px">Current classification</h3><div class="finding"><strong>Later framework built from biblical material.</strong><br>Not established as a formula taught by Jesus.</div></aside></div></section><section class="section" id="challenge"><div class="wrap"><div class="card"><div class="eyebrow">Open Challenge</div><h2>Can you overturn this?</h2><p class="muted">Attack the source, date, translation, context, inference, missing evidence, or hidden assumption. Trust-Worthy is required to investigate a serious challenge rather than defend its pride.</p><form class="challenge" action="/truth/challenge-submit.php" method="post"><input type="hidden" name="case_id" value="<?php echo $caseId; ?>"><input type="hidden" name="opened_at" id="openedAt" value=""><div class="hp" aria-hidden="true"><label>Website<input name="website" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off"></label></div><div class="row"><div><label for="challenge_type">Challenge type</label><select id="challenge_type" name="challenge_type" required><option value="">Choose one</option><option value="source">Source / provenance</option><option value="date">Date / chronology</option><option value="translation">Translation / language</option><option value="context">Context</option><option value="logic">Logic / inference</option><option value="counterevidence">Counterevidence</option><option value="assumption">Hidden assumption</option><option value="other">Other</option></select></div><div><label for="name">Name or handle</label><input id="name" name="name" maxlength="100"></div></div><label for="argument">Your challenge</label><textarea id="argument" name="argument" minlength="20" maxlength="6000" required></textarea><label for="source">Source or evidence</label><textarea id="source" name="source" maxlength="2000"></textarea><label for="email">Email (optional)</label><input id="email" type="email" name="email" maxlength="190"><button class="button" type="submit">Submit Evidence Challenge</button></form></div></div></section></main><footer class="footer"><div class="wrap">Trust-Worthy AI · Don't trust the answer. Examine the evidence.</div></footer><script>document.getElementById('openedAt').value=Math.floor(Date.now()/1000);</script></body></html>